WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.430
So, in this section we are going to talk
about genome assembly using read pairs.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:12.170
Pavel gave great lectures explaining how
to construct de Bruijn graph from reads.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.700
And from the obtained de Bruijn graph he
explained how to

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.429
find an Eulerian path in this graph to
identify the genome.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.970
But he forgot to tell you one important
thing.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:25.600
There can be

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.740
multiple Eulerian paths in the de Bruijn
graph.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:34.320
Even in this single de Bruijn graph, there
are two Eulerian paths in this graph.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.340
Here they are.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.850
And these two Eulerian paths correspond to
two different strings.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.760
Which string are you going to give to
biologists,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.520
the left one, or the right one, or both?

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.000
Please note that

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.180
we sequenced only one genome.
Well, this ambiguity

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.880
forces us to decompose the de Bruijn graph
into multiple disconnected components.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.270
Each component corresponds to a
non-branching path in the de Bruijn graph.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.550
Please note that while there are multiple
Eulerian

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.130
paths in the de Bruijn graph, all of them

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.150
share the same set of non-branching paths
shown here.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.340
These non-branching paths in their turn
correspond

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.060
to contigs, which are substrings in the
genome.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:37.360
Well, ideally, biologists would like to
have one contig for each assembly.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.420
Or at least, one should significantly
reduce

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.380
the number of contigs. To obtain this goal.
they

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.740
invented the technology called read pair
sequencing that I'm going to talk about.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:57.060
Given multiple identical copies of the
same genome, we now

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.130
randomly cut these genomes into large,
equally sized fragments

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:07.560
of size insertLength.
Now for each fragment we generate

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.000
pair of reads from both ends of each
fragment.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.350
So now instead of talking about reads
individually, we are talking

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:22.169
about pairs of reads separated by a
distance called insert size.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.830
So, given two k-mers, if they are at a
fixed distance

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.060
d apart in the genome, we call them a
paired k-mer.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:40.140
So the question is, how to construct the
genome, not from the k-mer

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:46.740
composition, like previously described, but
from paired k-mer composition.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.630
But what is paired k-mer composition?

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.640
Given a string, a paired k-mer composition
of a string

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.490
is a set of all paired k-mers generated
from this string.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.152
Well, because we don't have space, let's
represent a

00:03:03.152 --> 00:03:06.350
pair of k-mers as a two-line expression
like this.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:06.850
Okay?

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.956
We can order these paired k-mers in this
order, which is

00:03:13.956 --> 00:03:19.369
the order that they were generated from
the genome.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.290
But please note that the position
information is not available.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.820
So a correct way to represent the paired
k-mer

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:32.880
composition should be in lexicographic
order, like in a dictionary.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:41.780
So the question now is, can you construct
the string

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:48.380
given its paired k-mer composition.
Or let me describe formally.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:54.130
Input is a collection of paired k-mers.
Output is a string text such that

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:59.672
the paired k-mer composition of text is
equal to the collection of paired k-mers.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.800
How would you solve this?

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.810
Let's ask de Bruijn how would he solve
this problem.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:12.260
Well, I think that de Bruijn would came up
with the same approach

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:17.009
that I am going to describe right now,
which is the paired de Bruijn graph.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:24.980
Given a genome and its paired k-mer
composition, it's

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:30.140
clear that the genome can be represented
as a path, where

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.830
the edges are labeled by paired k-mers,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.344
and the nodes can be labeled by pairs of (k-1)-mers.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.520
And this path, we call it genome path.
But allow me

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.381
to do one crazy operation with this genome
path.

00:04:48.381 --> 00:04:55.470
Let me glue nodes with identical labels
together.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.436
In this slide, we have two nodes with
identical label, TG|AT.

00:05:00.436 --> 00:05:02.020
We glue them.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:08.110
We glue, and this graph is called the
paired de Bruijn graph from the genome.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.740
Now, you may guess that the next question
I'm going

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:18.570
to ask is, can you construct the paired de
Bruijn graph,

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:24.110
not from the genome, but from the paired
k-mer composition of the genome.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.225
Well, the answer is ..., let's see how we solve
this problem.

00:05:30.225 --> 00:05:34.325
Let's represent each paired

00:05:34.325 --> 00:05:37.470
k-mer by an edge,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.310
from the paired prefix to the paired
suffix.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.520
Now, let's do the same thing as in de Bruijn
graphs.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.690
We glue nodes with identical labels
together.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.470
Here we glue,

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:04.910
we glue, continue gluing, continue,
continue, continue.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:05.540
What is it?

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.810
This is the genome path,
as if the genome was available.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.290
But it's not all.
We have something more to glue.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:21.020
TG|AT, TG|AT, they are two nodes
with identical labels.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:23.620
We have to glue them, we glue.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.520
This is the paired de Bruijn graph from
the read pairs.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.740
Okay, to recap, the paired de Bruijn graph
for a

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:37.590
collection of paired k-mers can be
constructed by two operations.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.670
First, represent every paired k-mer as an

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:44.810
edge between its paired prefix and paired
suffix.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.930
Second, glue all nodes with identical
labels.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.870
In the paired de Bruijn graph, the genome
is

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:58.200
an Eulerian walk in the paired de Bruijn
graph.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:01.890
But what is the point?

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.890
In the de Bruijn graph, we also have this
property.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:09.720
The genome is also an Eulerian walk 
in the de Bruijn graph.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.288
But please note that in the paired de
Bruijn graph, nodes are labeled

00:07:16.288 --> 00:07:18.690
by pairs of (k-1)-mers,

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.511
while in the de Bruijn graph, nodes are

00:07:21.511 --> 00:07:25.080
labeled by just individual (k-1)-mers. 

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:32.500
Both of these graphs can be obtained by
gluing operations on the genome path.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:37.900
But because of the difference in the
labeling, paired de Bruijn

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:42.920
graph is obtained by fewer gluings and

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.800
that makes it simpler.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.100
In this case you can see that the paired

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.650
de Bruijn graph is simpler than the de
Bruijn graph.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:54.422
Indeed there's only one Eulerian path in
the paired de

00:07:54.422 --> 00:07:59.320
Bruijn graph that allows us to uniquely
construct the genome.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:03.940
While in the de Bruijn graph we cannot
construct the genome uniquely.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:08.560
In practice, paired de Bruijn graph has
been applied in assemblers and

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.810
it has shown significant advantages in the
resulting assembly.

